/-!
# Induction from a covering family of subgroups spans the class functions

Let `𝒮` be a family of subgroups of a finite group `G` that **covers `G` up to conjugacy**: every
element of `G` has a conjugate lying in some member of `𝒮`.  Over a field `k` in which `|G|` is
invertible, induction from `𝒮` is then surjective on class functions: the class functions
`Ind_S^G ψ`, for `S ∈ 𝒮` and `ψ` a class function of `S`, span all class functions of `G`
(`TauCeti.ClassFunction.indSpan_eq_top`).

The proof is the classical duality argument.  The character pairing is nondegenerate, so the span is
everything as soon as nothing nonzero is orthogonal to it.  Frobenius reciprocity converts
orthogonality to *every* class function induced from `S` into the vanishing of the restriction of
`h` to `S`, and a class function whose restriction to every member of `𝒮` vanishes is zero, because
each `x` is conjugate into some member and a class function is constant on conjugacy classes.

The cyclic subgroups cover `G`, for the trivial reason that `x` generates a cyclic subgroup
containing it, so the class functions induced from them span
(`TauCeti.ClassFunction.indSpan_cyclic_eq_top`).  The covering hypothesis is stated for a general
family because the elementary subgroups, which Brauer's induction theorem uses, satisfy it too.

Over an algebraically closed `k` the spanning set can be cut down to genuine induced *characters*:
the irreducible characters of `S` span the class functions of `S`, so
`TauCeti.ClassFunction.indCharacterSpan_cyclic_eq_top` says that the characters induced from the
irreducible characters of the cyclic subgroups already span.

## Relation to Artin's induction theorem

Artin's induction theorem is the sharper *arithmetic* statement, about the virtual-character
lattice `TauCeti.virtualCharacters` rather than about the class functions: over a
characteristic-zero splitting field, `|G| • χ` is a `ℤ`-linear combination of characters induced
from cyclic subgroups.  The rational form says that every character is a `ℚ`-linear combination
of them — equivalently, that some nonzero integer multiple is an integral combination — or that
`⨁_{C cyclic} R(C) → R(G)` is surjective after tensoring with `ℚ`.  The `|G|` statement is strictly
sharper.

This file supplies the duality half of that development, run on the class functions instead of on
`ℚ ⊗ R(G)`, and the family-general comparison between the integral induced-character span and
the canonical subgroup of induced virtual characters.  Over an algebraically closed field in which
the selected subgroup orders are invertible,
`TauCeti.ClassFunction.indVirtualCharacters_eq_indCharacterSpanInt` identifies those two integral
targets.  `TauCeti.RepresentationTheory.Induction.Artin` supplies the arithmetic half: it proves the
sharp `|G|` statement, specializes this comparison to its conventional induced-character-span
endpoint, and bundles the rationally surjective map after tensoring with `ℚ`.  Brauer's induction
theorem and Brauer's characterization of characters remain open.  The covering-family formulation
is stated here for its own sake: the elementary subgroups Brauer's theorem uses satisfy the same
hypothesis.

## Main definitions

* `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indSpan`: the submodule of class functions of `G` spanned by the class
  functions induced from a family `𝒮` of subgroups.
* `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indCharacterSpan`: the smaller submodule generated by inductions of only
  irreducible characters of the members of `𝒮`.
* `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indCharacterSpanInt`: the additive subgroup generated by the same
  induced characters, with integer coefficients.

## Main statements

* `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indSpan_eq_top`: induction from a family of subgroups covering `G` up
  to conjugacy spans all class functions, and
  `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indSpan_cyclic_eq_top`: its cyclic case, with
  `TauCeti.ClassFunction.exists_sum_smul_ind_cyclic` the explicit finite-combination form.
* `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indCharacterSpan_eq_indSpan`: over an algebraically closed field in which
  the orders of the selected subgroups are invertible,
  inducing only the irreducible characters of the subgroups spans as much as inducing all their
  class functions, whence `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indCharacterSpan_eq_top` and
  `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indCharacterSpan_cyclic_eq_top`, with
  `TauCeti.ClassFunction.exists_sum_smul_ind_ofCharacter_cyclic` the explicit form.
* `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indVirtualCharacters_eq_indCharacterSpanInt`: over an algebraically
  closed field in which the selected subgroup orders are invertible, the canonical subgroup of
  induced virtual characters equals the integral span of induced irreducible characters.

## Implementation notes

Only the invertibility of `|G|` in `k` is assumed for the first form; no algebraic closure is
needed, because the duality argument runs on
`TauCeti.ClassFunction.characterPairing_nondegenerate` alone.  Algebraic closure enters only in the
refinement to induced characters, where completeness of the irreducible characters of the subgroup
is used.  That refinement needs no assumption on `|G|` at all, only invertibility of the orders of
the members of `𝒮`, so it takes that as an explicit hypothesis rather than an ambient instance; the
covering corollaries supply it from `TauCeti.isUnit_natCard_subgroup`.

The covering hypothesis is written out as `∀ x : G, ∃ S ∈ 𝒮, ∃ y : G, y * x * y⁻¹ ∈ S` rather than
bundled into a predicate: it is used once, and the cyclic instance discharges it with `y = 1`.

The class-function spanning theorems produce coefficients in `k`.  In contrast,
`TauCeti.ClassFunction.indCharacterSpanInt` and its comparison with induced virtual characters are
statements about additive subgroups and therefore integral coefficients; see the discussion above
of what still separates these structural results from Artin's induction theorem.

## References

This is the duality step of the "Artin's induction theorem" item of Layer 6 in
`TauCetiRoadmap/RepresentationTheory/InductionRestriction/README.md`, which asks for the
`ℤ`-membership of `|G| • χ` in the subgroup generated by cyclic-induced characters and, separately,
for the rational surjectivity of `⨁_{C cyclic} R(C) → R(G)`.  Both are proved in
`TauCeti.RepresentationTheory.Induction.Artin` through the canonical induced-virtual-character API.

* J.-P. Serre, *Linear Representations of Finite Groups*, Springer GTM 42 (1977), Section 9.2,
  Theorem 17, whose duality argument this follows.  Serre runs it on `ℚ ⊗ R(G)`, which is what
  makes his conclusion the rational one; here it runs on the class functions over `k`.
* C. W. Curtis, I. Reiner, *Methods of Representation Theory, Vol. I*, Wiley (1981), Section 15.
-/

public section

namespace TauCeti

namespace ClassFunction

universe u v

variable (k : Type u) (G : Type v) [Field k] [Group G] [Finite G]

/-! ### Class functions induced from a family of subgroups -/

/-- The set of class functions of `G` induced from the members of a family `𝒮` of subgroups. -/
def indSet (𝒮 : Set (Subgroup G)) : Set (ClassFunction k G) :=
  {f | ∃ S ∈ 𝒮, ∃ ψ : ClassFunction k S, ind S ψ = f}

/-- The submodule generated by `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indSet`. -/
noncomputable def indSpan (𝒮 : Set (Subgroup G)) : Submodule k (ClassFunction k G) :=
  Submodule.span k (indSet k G 𝒮)

/-- Membership in the generating set for `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indSpan`. -/
@[simp]
theorem mem_indSet {𝒮 : Set (Subgroup G)} {f : ClassFunction k G} :
    f ∈ indSet k G 𝒮 ↔ ∃ S ∈ 𝒮, ∃ ψ : ClassFunction k S, ind S ψ = f :=
  Iff.rfl

variable {k G}

/-- A class function induced from a member of `𝒮` lies in `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indSpan`, the
submodule these generate. -/
theorem ind_mem_indSpan {𝒮 : Set (Subgroup G)} {S : Subgroup G} (hS : S ∈ 𝒮)
    (ψ : ClassFunction k S) :
    ind S ψ ∈ indSpan k G 𝒮 :=
  Submodule.subset_span ⟨S, hS, ψ, rfl⟩

/-- The universal property of `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indSpan`: it is contained in a submodule
exactly when every class function induced from a member of `𝒮` is. -/
theorem indSpan_le_iff {𝒮 : Set (Subgroup G)} {p : Submodule k (ClassFunction k G)} :
    indSpan k G 𝒮 ≤ p ↔ ∀ S ∈ 𝒮, ∀ ψ : ClassFunction k S, ind S ψ ∈ p := by
  refine ⟨fun h S hS ψ => h (ind_mem_indSpan hS ψ), fun h => Submodule.span_le.mpr ?_⟩
  rintro - ⟨S, hS, ψ, rfl⟩
  exact h S hS ψ

/-- **A class function orthogonal to every induction from `𝒮` vanishes**, when `𝒮` covers `G` up to
conjugacy.  Frobenius reciprocity turns orthogonality to all of `Ind_S^G (ClassFunction k S)` into
orthogonality of the restriction of `h` to `S` against all class functions of `S`, so that
restriction is zero by nondegeneracy of the pairing on `S`.  Any conjugate of `x` lying in `S` then
evaluates `h` at `x`. -/
theorem eq_zero_of_characterPairing_indSpan_eq_zero [Invertible (Nat.card G : k)]
    {𝒮 : Set (Subgroup G)}
    (h𝒮 : ∀ x : G, ∃ S ∈ 𝒮, ∃ y : G, y * x * y⁻¹ ∈ S) {h : ClassFunction k G}
    (hh : ∀ f ∈ indSpan k G 𝒮,
      (@characterPairing k G _ _ (Fintype.ofFinite G)) f h = 0) :
    h = 0 := by
  classical
  let _ := Fintype.ofFinite G
  have hG : IsUnit (Nat.card G : k) := isUnit_of_invertible _
  refine Subtype.ext (funext fun x => ?_)
  obtain ⟨S, hS, y, hy⟩ := h𝒮 x
  have hres : comap S.subtype h = 0 := by
    let : Invertible (Nat.card S : k) := (isUnit_natCard_subgroup _ hG).invertible
    refine characterPairing_nondegenerate.2 _ fun ψ => ?_
    rw [← characterPairing_ind hG ψ h]
    exact hh _ (ind_mem_indSpan hS ψ)
  have hzero := congrArg (fun f : ClassFunction k S => f.1 ⟨y * x * y⁻¹, hy⟩) hres
  rw [← mem_iff.mp h.2 x y]
  simpa using hzero

/-- **Induction from a covering family of subgroups is surjective on class functions.**  If every
element of the finite group `G` is conjugate into a member of `𝒮`, and `|G|` is invertible in `k`,
then the class functions induced from the members of `𝒮` span all class functions of `G`.

`TauCeti.ClassFunction.indSpan_cyclic_eq_top` is the case of the cyclic subgroups; the family of
elementary subgroups, which Brauer's induction theorem uses, satisfies the same hypothesis. -/
theorem indSpan_eq_top [Invertible (Nat.card G : k)] {𝒮 : Set (Subgroup G)}
    (h𝒮 : ∀ x : G, ∃ S ∈ 𝒮, ∃ y : G, y * x * y⁻¹ ∈ S) :
    indSpan k G 𝒮 = ⊤ := by
  let := Fintype.ofFinite G
  have hrefl := (characterPairing_isSymm (k := k) (G := G)).isRefl
  have hbot : (characterPairing (k := k) (G := G)).orthogonal (indSpan k G 𝒮) = ⊥ :=
    (Submodule.eq_bot_iff _).mpr fun _ hh =>
      eq_zero_of_characterPairing_indSpan_eq_zero h𝒮 fun f hf =>
        LinearMap.BilinForm.mem_orthogonal_iff.mp hh f hf
  calc indSpan k G 𝒮
      = (characterPairing (k := k) (G := G)).orthogonal
          ((characterPairing (k := k) (G := G)).orthogonal (indSpan k G 𝒮)) :=
        (LinearMap.BilinForm.orthogonal_orthogonal characterPairing_nondegenerate hrefl _).symm
    _ = ⊤ := by rw [hbot, LinearMap.BilinForm.orthogonal_bot]

/-- **The class functions induced from the cyclic subgroups span.**  Over a field in which the order
of the finite group `G` is invertible, the class functions induced from the cyclic subgroups of `G`
span all class functions of `G`; equivalently, the induction map
`⨁_{C cyclic} ClassFunction k C → ClassFunction k G` is surjective.

The explicit finite-combination form is `TauCeti.ClassFunction.exists_sum_smul_ind_cyclic`, and
`TauCeti.ClassFunction.indCharacterSpan_cyclic_eq_top` refines the spanning set to induced
characters.

This is *not* Artin's induction theorem: the coefficients here are arbitrary elements of `k`, while
Artin's theorem is a statement about `TauCeti.virtualCharacters`, asserting that they may be taken
rational and that `|G| • χ` is an integral combination of induced virtual characters.  Both forms
are proved in `TauCeti.RepresentationTheory.Induction.Artin`. -/
theorem indSpan_cyclic_eq_top [Invertible (Nat.card G : k)] :
    indSpan k G {S | IsCyclic S} = ⊤ :=
  indSpan_eq_top fun x =>
    ⟨Subgroup.zpowers x, Subgroup.isCyclic_zpowers x, 1, by simp⟩

/-- `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indSpan_cyclic_eq_top` written out: every class function of `G` is a
finite `k`-linear combination of class functions induced from cyclic subgroups. -/
theorem exists_sum_smul_ind_cyclic [Invertible (Nat.card G : k)] (f : ClassFunction k G) :
    ∃ (n : ℕ) (C : Fin n → Subgroup G) (_ : ∀ i, IsCyclic (C i))
      (ψ : ∀ i, ClassFunction k (C i)) (c : Fin n → k),
      ∑ i, c i • ind (C i) (ψ i) = f := by
  have hf : f ∈ indSpan k G {S | IsCyclic S} :=
    (indSpan_cyclic_eq_top (k := k) (G := G)).symm ▸ Submodule.mem_top
  obtain ⟨n, c, g, hg⟩ := Submodule.mem_span_set'.mp hf
  choose C hC ψ hψ using fun i => (g i).2
  exact ⟨n, C, hC, ψ, c, by simpa only [hψ] using hg⟩

/-! ### Characters induced from a family of subgroups -/

variable (k G)

/-- The set of characters induced from the irreducible characters of the members of a family `𝒮`
of subgroups of `G`. -/
def indCharacterSet (𝒮 : Set (Subgroup G)) : Set (ClassFunction k G) :=
  {f | ∃ S ∈ 𝒮, ∃ (n : ℕ) (ρ : Representation k S (Fin n → k)),
    ρ.IsIrreducible ∧ ind S (ofCharacter ρ) = f}

/-- The submodule of class functions of `G` spanned by the characters induced from the irreducible
characters of the members of a family `𝒮` of subgroups of `G`.

This is contained in `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indSpan`, and over an algebraically closed field in
which the orders of the selected subgroups are invertible the two agree
(`TauCeti.ClassFunction.indCharacterSpan_eq_indSpan`).

As in `TauCeti.ClassFunction.le_span_irreducibleCharacters`, the irreducibles are taken on the
coordinate spaces `Fin n → k`, which is where the Wedderburn presentation of the group algebra
produces them. -/
noncomputable def indCharacterSpan (𝒮 : Set (Subgroup G)) : Submodule k (ClassFunction k G) :=
  Submodule.span k (indCharacterSet k G 𝒮)

/-- Membership in the generating set for `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indCharacterSpan`. -/
@[simp]
theorem mem_indCharacterSet {𝒮 : Set (Subgroup G)} {f : ClassFunction k G} :
    f ∈ indCharacterSet k G 𝒮 ↔
      ∃ S ∈ 𝒮, ∃ (n : ℕ) (ρ : Representation k S (Fin n → k)),
        ρ.IsIrreducible ∧ ind S (ofCharacter ρ) = f :=
  Iff.rfl

variable {k G}

/-- A character induced from an irreducible character of a member of `𝒮` lies in
`TauCeti.ClassFunction.indCharacterSpan`, the submodule these generate. -/
theorem ind_ofCharacter_mem_indCharacterSpan {𝒮 : Set (Subgroup G)} {S : Subgroup G}
    (hS : S ∈ 𝒮) {n : ℕ} (ρ : Representation k S (Fin n → k)) (hρ : ρ.IsIrreducible) :
    ind S (ofCharacter ρ) ∈ indCharacterSpan k G 𝒮 :=
  Submodule.subset_span ⟨S, hS, n, ρ, hρ, rfl⟩

/-- The universal property of `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indCharacterSpan`: it is contained in a
submodule exactly when every character induced from an irreducible character of a member of `𝒮`
is. -/
theorem indCharacterSpan_le_iff {𝒮 : Set (Subgroup G)}
    {p : Submodule k (ClassFunction k G)} :
    indCharacterSpan k G 𝒮 ≤ p ↔ ∀ S ∈ 𝒮, ∀ (n : ℕ) (ρ : Representation k S (Fin n → k)),
      ρ.IsIrreducible → ind S (ofCharacter ρ) ∈ p := by
  refine ⟨fun h S hS n ρ hρ => h (ind_ofCharacter_mem_indCharacterSpan hS ρ hρ),
    fun h => Submodule.span_le.mpr ?_⟩
  rintro - ⟨S, hS, n, ρ, hρ, rfl⟩
  exact h S hS n ρ hρ

variable (k G)

/-- The additive subgroup of functions on `G` generated by characters induced from irreducible
characters of the subgroups satisfying `P`.  This is the integral-lattice counterpart
of `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indCharacterSpan`; unlike that `k`-linear span, its coefficients are
integers. -/
def indCharacterSpanInt (P : Subgroup G → Prop) : AddSubgroup (G → k) :=
  AddSubgroup.closure
    {f | ∃ χ ∈ indCharacterSet k G {C | P C}, (χ : G → k) = f}

variable {k G}

/-- A character induced from an irreducible character of a subgroup satisfying `P` belongs to
`TauCeti.ClassFunction.indCharacterSpanInt`. -/
theorem ind_ofCharacter_mem_indCharacterSpanInt {P : Subgroup G → Prop} (C : Subgroup G)
    (hC : P C)
    {n : ℕ} (ρ : Representation k C (Fin n → k)) (hρ : ρ.IsIrreducible) :
    ((ind C (ofCharacter ρ) : ClassFunction k G) : G → k) ∈
      indCharacterSpanInt k G P :=
  AddSubgroup.subset_closure ⟨ind C (ofCharacter ρ),
    (mem_indCharacterSet k G).2 ⟨C, hC, n, ρ, hρ, rfl⟩, rfl⟩

/-- The universal property of `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indCharacterSpanInt`: it is contained
in an additive subgroup exactly when every generating induced irreducible character is. -/
theorem indCharacterSpanInt_le_iff {P : Subgroup G → Prop} {H : AddSubgroup (G → k)} :
    indCharacterSpanInt k G P ≤ H ↔
      ∀ (C : Subgroup G), P C → ∀ (n : ℕ) (ρ : Representation k C (Fin n → k)),
        ρ.IsIrreducible → ((ind C (ofCharacter ρ) : ClassFunction k G) : G → k) ∈ H := by
  rw [indCharacterSpanInt, AddSubgroup.closure_le]
  constructor
  · intro h C hC n ρ hρ
    exact h ⟨ind C (ofCharacter ρ),
      (mem_indCharacterSet k G).2 ⟨C, hC, n, ρ, hρ, rfl⟩, rfl⟩
  · rintro h - ⟨χ, hχ, rfl⟩
    obtain ⟨C, hC, n, ρ, hρ, rfl⟩ := (mem_indCharacterSet k G).1 hχ
    exact h C hC n ρ hρ

/-- A character induced from an irreducible character of a subgroup satisfying `P` is induced from
a virtual character of that subgroup, so the induced-irreducible span sits inside the canonical
subgroup induced from the family. -/
theorem indCharacterSpanInt_le_indVirtualCharacters {P : Subgroup G → Prop} :
    indCharacterSpanInt k G P ≤ indVirtualCharacters k G P :=
  indCharacterSpanInt_le_iff.mpr fun C hC _ ρ _ => by
    have hof : (ofCharacter ρ : ClassFunction k C) = ofFDRep (FDRep.of ρ) :=
      (ofFDRep_eq_ofCharacter (FDRep.of ρ)).symm
    have hchar : ((ofFDRep (FDRep.of ρ) : ClassFunction k C) : C → k) = (FDRep.of ρ).character :=
      funext (ofFDRep_apply _)
    have hmem : ((ofCharacter ρ : ClassFunction k C) : C → k) ∈ virtualCharacters k C := by
      rw [hof, hchar]
      exact character_mem_virtualCharacters _
    have hind : ((ind C (ofCharacter ρ) : ClassFunction k G) : G → k) =
        indClassFun C ((ofCharacter ρ : ClassFunction k C) : C → k) :=
      funext fun g => ind_apply _ g
    rw [hind]
    exact indClassFun_mem_indVirtualCharacters hC hmem

/-- **The virtual characters induced from a family are generated by induced irreducible
characters.** Over an algebraically closed field in which each selected subgroup order is
invertible, a virtual character of a subgroup `C` is an integer combination of its irreducible
characters
(`TauCeti.virtualCharacters_eq_closure_irreducibleCharacters`), and induction is additive, so
inducing all of `R(C)` produces nothing beyond what inducing the irreducible characters produces. -/
theorem indVirtualCharacters_le_indCharacterSpanInt [IsAlgClosed k]
    {P : Subgroup G → Prop} (hP : ∀ C, P C → IsUnit (Nat.card C : k)) :
    indVirtualCharacters k G P ≤ indCharacterSpanInt k G P := by
  rw [indVirtualCharacters_eq_closure_indFDRepCharacter, AddSubgroup.closure_le]
  rintro _ ⟨C, hC, V, rfl⟩
  let _ : Invertible (Nat.card C : k) := (hP C hC).invertible
  have hle : AddSubgroup.closure (irreducibleCharacters k C) ≤
      (indCharacterSpanInt k G P).comap (indClassFunAddHom C) := by
    rw [AddSubgroup.closure_le]
    rintro χ hχ
    obtain ⟨n, ρ, hρ, rfl⟩ := mem_irreducibleCharacters_iff.mp hχ
    have hof : ((ofCharacter ρ : ClassFunction k C) : C → k) = ρ.character :=
      funext (ofCharacter_apply ρ)
    have hind : indClassFun C ρ.character =
        ((ind C (ofCharacter ρ) : ClassFunction k G) : G → k) := by
      rw [← hof]
      exact (funext fun g => ind_apply _ g).symm
    rw [SetLike.mem_coe, AddSubgroup.mem_comap, indClassFunAddHom_apply, hind]
    exact ind_ofCharacter_mem_indCharacterSpanInt C hC ρ hρ
  have hmem := hle (character_mem_closure_irreducibleCharacters V)
  rwa [AddSubgroup.mem_comap, indClassFunAddHom_apply] at hmem

/-- **The canonical induced-virtual-character subgroup is generated by induced irreducible
characters.** Over an algebraically closed field in which each selected subgroup order is
invertible, it makes no difference, for any family `P`, whether one induces the irreducible
characters of its members or all of their virtual characters. -/
theorem indVirtualCharacters_eq_indCharacterSpanInt [IsAlgClosed k]
    {P : Subgroup G → Prop} (hP : ∀ C, P C → IsUnit (Nat.card C : k)) :
    indVirtualCharacters k G P = indCharacterSpanInt k G P :=
  le_antisymm (indVirtualCharacters_le_indCharacterSpanInt hP)
    indCharacterSpanInt_le_indVirtualCharacters

/-- A character is a class function, so inducing only the irreducible characters of the members of
`𝒮` spans no more than inducing all their class functions. -/
theorem indCharacterSpan_le_indSpan (𝒮 : Set (Subgroup G)) :
    indCharacterSpan k G 𝒮 ≤ indSpan k G 𝒮 :=
  indCharacterSpan_le_iff.mpr fun _ hS _ ρ _ => ind_mem_indSpan hS (ofCharacter ρ)

/-- Inducing an arbitrary class function of a subgroup gains nothing over inducing its irreducible
characters: over an algebraically closed field the latter already span the class functions of the
subgroup, and induction is linear.

Only the orders of the *selected* subgroups need be invertible in `k`; `|G|` itself is irrelevant
here, and the covering corollaries below supply the hypothesis from
`TauCeti.isUnit_natCard_subgroup`. -/
theorem indSpan_le_indCharacterSpan [IsAlgClosed k] {𝒮 : Set (Subgroup G)}
    (h𝒮 : ∀ S ∈ 𝒮, IsUnit (Nat.card S : k)) :
    indSpan k G 𝒮 ≤ indCharacterSpan k G 𝒮 := by
  refine indSpan_le_iff.mpr fun S hS ψ => ?_
  let : Invertible (Nat.card S : k) := (h𝒮 S hS).invertible
  let := Fintype.ofFinite (ConjClasses S)
  obtain ⟨d, ρ, b, hirr, hb⟩ := exists_basis_ofCharacter k S
  have hsum : ψ = ∑ D, b.repr ψ D • ofCharacter (ρ D) := by
    conv_lhs => rw [← b.sum_repr ψ]
    exact Finset.sum_congr rfl fun D _ => by rw [hb D]
  rw [hsum, map_sum]
  refine Submodule.sum_mem _ fun D _ => ?_
  rw [map_smul]
  exact Submodule.smul_mem _ _
    (ind_ofCharacter_mem_indCharacterSpan hS (ρ D) (hirr D))

/-- Over an algebraically closed field, inducing the irreducible characters of the members of `𝒮`
spans exactly as much as inducing all their class functions.  As in
`TauCeti.ClassFunction.indSpan_le_indCharacterSpan`, only the orders of the members of `𝒮`
need be invertible. -/
theorem indCharacterSpan_eq_indSpan [IsAlgClosed k] {𝒮 : Set (Subgroup G)}
    (h𝒮 : ∀ S ∈ 𝒮, IsUnit (Nat.card S : k)) :
    indCharacterSpan k G 𝒮 = indSpan k G 𝒮 :=
  le_antisymm (indCharacterSpan_le_indSpan 𝒮) (indSpan_le_indCharacterSpan h𝒮)

/-- **Induction of irreducible characters from a covering family of subgroups already spans the
class functions**, over an algebraically closed field in which `|G|` is invertible. -/
theorem indCharacterSpan_eq_top [IsAlgClosed k] [Invertible (Nat.card G : k)]
    {𝒮 : Set (Subgroup G)} (h𝒮 : ∀ x : G, ∃ S ∈ 𝒮, ∃ y : G, y * x * y⁻¹ ∈ S) :
    indCharacterSpan k G 𝒮 = ⊤ :=
  top_le_iff.mp ((indSpan_eq_top h𝒮).ge.trans
    (indSpan_le_indCharacterSpan fun S _ =>
      isUnit_natCard_subgroup S (isUnit_of_invertible _)))

/-- **The characters induced from the irreducible characters of the cyclic subgroups span.**  Over
an algebraically closed field in which the order of the finite group `G` is invertible, they span
all class functions of `G`.

In particular every character of `G` is a `k`-linear combination of characters induced from cyclic
subgroups; `TauCeti.ClassFunction.exists_sum_smul_ind_ofCharacter_cyclic` writes that combination
out. The coefficients are arbitrary elements of `k`. Artin's induction theorem asserts that they
may be taken rational; `TauCeti.ClassFunction.natCard_nsmul_mem_indVirtualCharacters_isCyclic`
gives its sharp integral form through the canonical virtual-character API. -/
theorem indCharacterSpan_cyclic_eq_top [IsAlgClosed k] [Invertible (Nat.card G : k)] :
    indCharacterSpan k G {S | IsCyclic S} = ⊤ :=
  indCharacterSpan_eq_top fun x =>
    ⟨Subgroup.zpowers x, Subgroup.isCyclic_zpowers x, 1, by simp⟩

/-- `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indCharacterSpan_cyclic_eq_top` written out over an algebraically closed
field: every class function of `G` — in particular, by `TauCeti.ClassFunction.ofCharacter`, every
character of `G` — is a finite `k`-linear combination of characters induced from irreducible
characters of cyclic subgroups. -/
theorem exists_sum_smul_ind_ofCharacter_cyclic [IsAlgClosed k] [Invertible (Nat.card G : k)]
    (f : ClassFunction k G) :
    ∃ (n : ℕ) (C : Fin n → Subgroup G) (_ : ∀ i, IsCyclic (C i)) (d : Fin n → ℕ)
      (ρ : ∀ i, Representation k (C i) (Fin (d i) → k)) (_ : ∀ i, (ρ i).IsIrreducible)
      (c : Fin n → k),
      ∑ i, c i • ind (C i) (ofCharacter (ρ i)) = f := by
  have hf : f ∈ indCharacterSpan k G {S | IsCyclic S} :=
    (indCharacterSpan_cyclic_eq_top (k := k) (G := G)).symm ▸ Submodule.mem_top
  obtain ⟨n, c, g, hg⟩ := Submodule.mem_span_set'.mp hf
  choose C hC d ρ hρ hfg using fun i => (g i).2
  exact ⟨n, C, hC, d, ρ, hρ, c, by simpa only [hfg] using hg⟩

end ClassFunction

end TauCeti
